  <h2>How a turn feels</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Wingspan</strong> gives you four actions: play a bird, gain food from the birdfeeder dice, lay
    eggs, or draw bird cards. Every bird you add to a row makes that row's action stronger, so the game is
    about building those rows up before the four rounds run out. You get 26 actions in the whole game
    (8, 7, 6 and 5 per round), and feeling that shrink is part of the tension.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Forest Shuffle</strong> gives you two choices: take two cards, or play one card by paying for it
    with other cards from your hand. Those payments go into the clearing in the middle, where other players
    can take them. Cards are your money and your forest at the same time, which is where most of the
    decisions come from. There are no rounds; the game ends the moment the third winter card is drawn.
  </p>

  <h2>Learning it</h2>
  <p>
    Forest Shuffle is quicker to start: the turn fits in one sentence. The work comes at the end, when
    every card in your forest scores by its own condition, and the first few games take a while to count.
    Wingspan has more moving parts to explain (food types, the birdfeeder, eggs, bonus cards, round goals),
    but the player mat and the icons do a lot of the teaching, and scoring is a quick tally.
  </p>

  <h2>At two players, and solo</h2>
  <p>
    Both work well at two. Forest Shuffle at two tends to run closer to 40 minutes, and you watch the
    clearing closely, because every card you pay with can help your opponent. Wingspan at two is calmer:
    you mostly watch each other's engines grow.
  </p>
  <p>
    For solo play, Wingspan wins out of the box: its Automa opponent is included. Forest Shuffle needs the
    <a href="/games/forest-shuffle/forest-shuffle-expansions/">Exploration expansion</a>, which adds an
    Automa deck with 12 challenges.
  </p>

  <h2>On the table and on the shelf</h2>
  <p>
    Wingspan takes more room: player mats, the birdfeeder dice tower, egg miniatures and food tokens. Forest
    Shuffle is mostly cards, so it suits smaller tables and travels better. Both boxes fill up once you
    add expansions. Forest Shuffle's three expansions all fit in the base game box, and our
    <a href="/products/forest-shuffle-insert/">insert</a> keeps all of it sorted, sleeved.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="tip" title="Already own Wingspan?">
    Then Forest Shuffle is the more interesting second game than a Wingspan expansion if your group wants
    something shorter with more back-and-forth. If they love Wingspan exactly as it is, an expansion or
    <a href="/games/wingspan/">upgrades for the components you handle every turn</a> may be the better buy.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>The verdict</h2>
  <p>
    Choose <strong>Wingspan</strong> for the bigger, more varied game with a satisfying build to round four,
    and for solo play without extras. Choose <strong>Forest Shuffle</strong> for a faster teach, a smaller
    footprint and tighter two-player games. Plenty of shelves have both, because they scratch different
    itches: Wingspan for a relaxed evening, Forest Shuffle for when you want to fight over the clearing.
  </p>
